WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.672
Secure Axum development is a layered
engineering practice.

2
00:00:04.752 --> 00:00:08.516
Start with the route map and the
trust-boundary model.

3
00:00:08.596 --> 00:00:12.195
A reviewer should be able to identify
public routes,

4
00:00:12.275 --> 00:00:17.102
authenticated routes, administrative
routes, internal callbacks,

5
00:00:17.182 --> 00:00:20.782
health endpoints, shared state, middleware
scope,

6
00:00:20.862 --> 00:00:23.971
and the request lifecycle without
guessing.

7
00:00:24.051 --> 00:00:26.996
Use extractors to structure request data,

8
00:00:27.076 --> 00:00:30.349
but do not confuse extraction with
validation.

9
00:00:30.429 --> 00:00:33.047
Validate business rules explicitly.

10
00:00:33.127 --> 00:00:38.772
Convert raw request values into trusted
domain values only after checks for type,

11
00:00:38.852 --> 00:00:43.515
range, tenant, ownership, resource state,
and operation context.

12
00:00:43.595 --> 00:00:48.422
Reject unsupported values safely before
they reach sensitive logic.

13
00:00:48.502 --> 00:00:52.020
Keep authentication and authorization
separate.

14
00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:55.045
Identity tells the service who is calling.

15
00:00:55.125 --> 00:01:01.015
Authorization decides whether that
identity may take a specific action on a specific

16
00:01:01.095 --> 00:01:01.832
resource.

17
00:01:01.912 --> 00:01:06.330
Fail closed when identity or authorization
context is missing,

18
00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:09.355
expired, inconsistent, or insufficient.

19
00:01:09.435 --> 00:01:14.262
Protect administrative and high-impact
actions with extra clarity.

20
00:01:14.342 --> 00:01:18.678
Map errors to safe responses, apply
middleware deliberately,

21
00:01:18.758 --> 00:01:23.012
review headers and CORS, configure body
limits and timeouts,

22
00:01:23.092 --> 00:01:25.383
and document proxy assumptions.

23
00:01:25.463 --> 00:01:30.126
Protect data access with authorization,
parameterized patterns,

24
00:01:30.206 --> 00:01:34.787
resource limits, connection-pool
awareness, timeout behavior,

25
00:01:34.867 --> 00:01:37.731
bounded concurrency, and backpressure.

26
00:01:37.811 --> 00:01:41.902
Finally, maintain the service as the
environment changes.

27
00:01:41.982 --> 00:01:44.436
Review dependencies and features.

28
00:01:44.516 --> 00:01:47.052
Protect secrets and configuration.

29
00:01:47.132 --> 00:01:50.732
Use structured logging and tracing with
redaction.

30
00:01:50.812 --> 00:01:53.921
Test expected and unexpected request
flows.

31
00:01:54.001 --> 00:01:56.128
Monitor production behavior.

32
00:01:56.208 --> 00:02:02.180
Improve the application as new risks,
dependencies, and operational lessons appear.
